Under the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), cases that can be tried summarily by a Magistrate include:

- Any Chief Judicial Magistrate or a Magistrate of the first class can try in a summary way certain offences such as:
    (i) Theft under sections 303(2), 305, or 306 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, where the value of the property stolen does not exceed twenty thousand rupees;
    (ii) Receiving or retaining stolen property under section 317(2) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, value not exceeding twenty thousand rupees;
    (iii) Assisting in the concealment or disposal of stolen property under section 317(5) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, value not exceeding twenty thousand rupees;
    (iv) Offences under sections 331(2) and (3) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023.

Additionally, a Magistrate of the second class may also be conferred powers by the High Court to try summarily any offence punishable only with fine or with imprisonment for a term not exceeding six months with or without fine, and any abetment of or attempt to commit such offences [BNSS | active | procedural | Section 283][BNSS | active | procedural | Section 284].

